<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radcliffe Line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1947</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Radcliffe Line is the boundary drawn in 1947 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, named after the British lawyer Sir Cyril Radcliffe, who chaired the two boundary commissions. Radcliffe had never been to India and had about five weeks for the task. The award was published on 17 August 1947, two days after independence, and the partition it fixed was accompanied by mass migration and communal violence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radcliffe-line.jpg" alt="Radcliffe Line" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partition_of_India_1947_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
